---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { states, kits, isEnacted, siteName, site, absUrl, prices, yields, fmtDate, lastCheckedAll } from '../lib/data';
import { organizationNode, editorNode, editorName, ORG_ID } from '../lib/ld';
const name = siteName;
const lawCount = states.filter(isEnacted).length;
const aboutLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@graph': [
    { '@type': 'AboutPage', '@id': `${absUrl('/about/')}#page`, url: absUrl('/about/'), name: `About ${name}`, mainEntity: { '@id': ORG_ID }, inLanguage: 'en-US' },
    organizationNode(),
    editorNode(),
  ],
};
---
<Base title="About" description={`${name} is an independent site run by ${editorName}, an independent researcher. It tracks US plug-in (balcony) solar laws and kit certification, checked against official sources.`} crumbs={[{ name: 'About', href: '/about/' }]} jsonLd={[aboutLd]}>
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>About {name}</h1>
    <p style="font-size:1.1rem"><strong>{name}</strong> is an independent site run by <strong>{editorName}</strong>, an independent researcher.</p>

    <h2>What the site does</h2>
    <p>Plug-in solar, also called balcony solar, means a small solar system that plugs into an ordinary outlet. {lawCount} states have now signed laws that say when you can use one without a utility interconnection agreement. Each law is different: size limits, notices to utilities and landlords, and the safety certification a kit needs.</p>
    <p>The site answers three questions for all 50 states and DC: is plug-in solar legal where you live, what are the rules, and which kits qualify? It also estimates what a kit could save, state by state. We track {kits.length} kits and the certificates behind them.</p>

    <h2>How every fact is checked</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>State laws</strong> come from official legislature texts: bill pages, enrolled and chaptered laws, governors' signing records and utility commission pages. Trackers and news only give us leads.</li>
      <li><strong>Kit certification</strong> counts only when we have seen a certifier's directory entry or a certificate document. A maker's claim is shown as a claim.</li>
      <li><strong>Solar output</strong> comes from {yields.model}, run for each state capital.</li>
      <li><strong>Electricity prices</strong> are U.S. EIA residential averages ({prices.period}).</li>
      <li><strong>Check dates on every page.</strong> Each state page, kit page and guide shows the date we last checked it, and every source shows the date we accessed it. The oldest state record was checked {fmtDate(lastCheckedAll())}.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>When we cannot confirm a fact, we say "unknown" instead of guessing. The full method is on <a href="/how-we-verify/">how we verify</a>.</p>

    <h2>Independence</h2>
    <p>We are not a government agency, a utility or a solar company, and we are not affiliated with any of them. Some retailer links may earn us a commission. Those links are labelled "Sponsored", and affiliate links never change a rating or a verdict. Brands cannot pay for a verdict. See our <a href="/disclosure/">disclosure</a>.</p>

    <h2>Corrections</h2>
    <p>If we get something wrong, we fix it and log the fix with its date. Read the <a href="/corrections/">corrections policy</a>.</p>

    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p>Corrections, questions and press: <strong>{site.CONTACT_EMAIL}</strong>. Journalists: see <a href="/press/">for journalists</a> and our <a href="/data/">data downloads</a>.</p>

    <h2>What we are not</h2>
    <p>We do not give legal or electrical advice. Read the <a href="/disclaimer/">disclaimer</a>.</p>
  </article>
</Base>
